Commit:
Commit just saves a snapshot of the project you are working in. (it doesn’t upload the project to github). git saves the history of the commits used before and when.

Git Branching:
These are pointers to the commits. They don’t take up any space.
“git checkout <name>” switches you to the branch named <name>. When you then run git commit, the new commit is added to that branch instead of main. This allows you to continue developing on the new branch without making commits directly to main.
Branch= creates the branch
checkout → switch to the branch
commit → save your changes on that branch

Git Merge:
Uses to merge two branches 
syntax= git merge <name>
Here the “<name>” branch is merged into the branch we are presently in.

Git Rebase:
makes your branch look like it was created from the latest main. Moves the branches work to the latest base. 
 
Git Head:
Shows what we currently have checked out.
“^” :
Leads you to the parent head.

“-f”:
Forces the operation. Be  careful with it as it has the power to discard the data.

Reversing:
Reset= will move a branch backward as if the commit never happened.
Revert= reverses changes and shares these reversed changes with others.

Cherry-pick:
takes one specific commit from another branch and applies it to current branch.
•	git cherry-pick <Commit1> <Commit2> <...>

-i:
git will open up a UI to show you which commits are about to be copied below the target of the rebase. It also shows their commit hashes and messages.

 Git add:
“git add” puts your changes into the staging area, meaning you are telling Git:
“Include these changes in my next commit.” Advantage of using “add” and then commit is to make the commits more specific.

Undo:
•	git restore --staged <file>: unstage a file (move it back out of the staging area, keeping your edits)
•	git restore <file>: discard your edits to a file entirely (careful, this throws the changes away!)

Locally stacked commits:
Use cherry pick or git rebase -i <name> thingys to do it
Git Clone:
Syntax: git clone <link>
Copy the link from the project on github. Copying it in the terminal will import all the files of the project 

Git switch:
Makes the branch and shifts you to it. More useful than using commit+checkout

Delete a branch:
git branch -d <name> deletes it

Git Push:
git push sends your local commits to the remote repository (GitHub)

git push:
gets the latest changes from githb and updates my local repository 

git fetch:
its smiliar to git pull but it doesn’t merge the files yet

git log:
git log --oneline — view commit history ( in compact way)
git log - gives Full commit history in expanded form

Git Stash:
git stash - saves changes temporarily allowing u to work on something else and come back at the same point.

Git Ignore:
.gitignore – empty folder - to avoid tracking the files present in this folder.
Username:
git config --global user.name "NAME" — set your Git username
git config --global user.email "EMAIL" — set your Git email

git --version – to check the version
